  "answer_text": "Because your request is brief, the term **\"Chase Capital Services\"** most likely refers to one of the following contexts depending on your location and situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chase Bank / Chase Auto Leases (United States)\nIn the U.S., **\"Chase Capital Services\"** is the legal lessor entity name listed on vehicle titles and DMV registrations for cars leased through **Chase Auto**. \n* **When you see this:** If you lease a vehicle through Chase (such as a Mazda, Subaru, Jaguar, Land Rover, or other partner manufacturers), \"Chase Capital Services\" (often registered in New York) is listed on the title as the owner of the vehicle.\n* **Lease Buyouts & DMV Transfers:** Many people search for this when trying to buy out their lease, request a clean title, or transfer a leased car's registration to a different state.\n* **How to contact them:** \n  * If you need a payoff quote, power of attorney for out-of-state DMV registration, or title release, you must contact **Chase Auto Leases** directly at **1-800-227-5151**.\n  * Fax number for DMV/title paperwork: **(937) 481-5324**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Chase Capital Services Private Limited (India)\nIf you are located in India, **Chase Capital Services Private Limited** is an independent, active financial services company incorporated in 1997.\n* **What they do:** They specialize in project consultancy, corporate advisory, debt syndication, securitization, and currency/commodity trading. \n* **Location:** They are based in Chandigarh and Mohali, Punjab, India. \n* *Note: This company is completely separate from JPMorgan Chase & Co. (the U.S. banking giant).*\n\n---\n\n### 3. Important Warning Regarding Financial Scams\nBecause \"Chase\" is a highly recognizable name, financial scammers often impersonate it to run fraudulent schemes:\n* **Unauthorized Loan Offers:** Scammers sometimes set up fraudulent websites or cold-call consumers using variations of this name (such as *\"Chase Capital Online\"* or *\"Chase Capital Services Loan\"*) targeting people with loan-fee fraud (where you are asked to pay an upfront \"processing fee\" or \"collateral\" to get a loan). \n* **Phishing:** Be cautious of emails or text messages claiming to be from \"Chase Capital Services\" asking for your banking details or to log into a link. \n\n*Always contact Chase Bank directly using the number on the back of your credit/debit card or via their official website (chase.com) rather than trusting unsolicited messages.*",
